description:
## Summary

`nf-blocks` is a Nextflow plugin that publishes pipeline outputs into a content-addressed store and records their lineage there. It owns the `cas` URI scheme, allowing users to configure `outputDir = 'cas://lab'` to send published files through the plugin instead of to a traditional `results/` directory tree. The plugin provides a lineage store for `lineage.store.location = 'cas://lab'`, so Nextflow's own `lid://` records live in the same store. It supports content-addressed storage with DAG-CBOR lineage records, SQLite indexing, and features like the `fromStore` channel factory for reading outputs in downstream pipelines and an explorer for browsing stored content and managing Selections.

**Status: beta** (version 0.11.0-beta.1). The plugin supports Milestone 4 (cloud) with local directories or S3 locations, Milestone 5 (nf-core pipelines) with workflow output lineage recording, and Milestone 6 (retention) with configurable content retention and garbage collection. It requires the `nf-amazon` plugin (≥3.9.2).

## Get Started

Enable the plugin and point both the lineage store and the output directory at the same store alias:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-blocks@0.11.0-beta.1&#x27;
}

lineage.enabled = true
lineage.store.location = &#x27;cas://lab&#x27;   // alias of the writable member
outputDir = &#x27;cas://lab&#x27;                // optional: unset, it is the lineage alias

cas {
    stores {
        lab {
            location = &#x27;/data/cas&#x27;     // the writable member
        }
    }
    resolve = [&#x27;lab&#x27;]                  // optional; default is every alias, writable first
    asserted_by = &#x27;ada@example.org&#x27;    // optional; who the blocks say asserted them
}
```

`asserted_by` is optional. Left unset, nf-blocks uses your Seqera Platform identity, then `NXF_BLOCKS_USER`, then `git config user.email`, and warns if none of them gives a name. A value of `anonymous` counts as unset. See [docs/identity.md](docs/identity.md) for the order and what each source reads.

An alias matches `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` and must not parse as a content address. `outputDir` may be left out: unset, it means the alias in `lineage.store.location`. When set, it must name that alias, or the run aborts.

Nextflow downloads the plugin from the Nextflow Registry the first time the pipeline runs.

To add nf-blocks to someone else's pipeline, such as an nf-core one, with a `-c` config file: a `plugins { }` block in that file replaces the pipeline's own plugin list rather than adding to it. Repeat the pipeline's pinned plugins beside nf-blocks, or Nextflow installs the latest release of each one the pipeline includes.

## Examples

Any pipeline with a workflow `output` block publishes into the store. With the configuration above, running a pipeline writes each published path under the store's coordinate tree:

```
/data/cas/coords/aligned/A/A.bam
/data/cas/coords/qc/A/A_qc/summary.txt
/data/cas/nf/&lt;run hash&gt;/aligned/A/A.bam/.data.json
```

The lineage record for a published file names its coordinate rather than a work directory path:

```json
{&quot;kind&quot;:&quot;FileOutput&quot;,&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;path&quot;:&quot;cas://lab/aligned/A/A.bam&quot;, ...}}
```

A second pipeline reads a run's outputs back out of the store with `fromStore`. Its config names the producer's store as a read-only member beside its own writable one:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-blocks@0.11.0-beta.1&#x27;
}

manifest.name = &#x27;downstream&#x27;

lineage.enabled = true
lineage.store.location = &#x27;cas://mine&#x27;

cas {
    stores {
        mine { location = &#x27;/data/cas-downstream&#x27; }
        lab  { location = &#x27;/data/cas&#x27; }
    }
    resolve = [&#x27;mine&#x27;, &#x27;lab&#x27;]
}
```

`fromStore` is used in a pipeline script:

```nextflow
include { fromStore } from &#x27;plugin/nf-blocks&#x27;

workflow {
    main:
    picked  = channel.fromStore(selection: &#x27;&lt;selection address&gt;&#x27;)
    aligned = channel.fromStore(run: &#x27;latest&#x27;, pipeline: &#x27;cas-test-pipeline&#x27;, output: &#x27;aligned&#x27;, where: [sample: &#x27;B&#x27;])

    picked.mix(aligned).view()
}
```

Each item arrives in the shape the producer published it, here `[meta, bam]`, with every file a `cas://` path Nextflow stages like any other.

For publishing into S3:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-blocks@0.11.0-beta.1&#x27;
}

lineage.enabled = true
lineage.store.location = &#x27;cas://lab&#x27;

workDir = &#x27;s3://my-work-bucket/work&#x27;
process.executor = &#x27;awsbatch&#x27;
process.queue = &#x27;my-queue&#x27;

aws {
    region = &#x27;us-east-1&#x27;
    profile = &#x27;my-profile&#x27;
}

cas {
    stores {
        lab { location = &#x27;s3://my-cas-bucket/cas&#x27; }
    }
    tmpDir = &#x27;/scratch/nf-blocks&#x27;
}
```

Browse a store with:

```bash
nextflow plugin nf-blocks:explore
```

This serves a live view of every configured member at `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`.

Command-line tools for managing content and retention:

```bash
nextflow plugin nf-blocks:items aligned nested.kit=truseq --run latest --pipeline cas-test-pipeline

nextflow plugin nf-blocks:prune --keep-last 3
nextflow plugin nf-blocks:prune --keep-last 3 --apply true

nextflow plugin nf-blocks:sweep
nextflow plugin nf-blocks:sweep --apply true

nextflow plugin nf-blocks:untrash &lt;cid&gt; [&lt;cid&gt; ...]
```

For typed scripts:

```nextflow
nextflow.enable.types = true

include { fromStore } from &#x27;plugin/nf-blocks&#x27;

record Sample {
    sample: String
    lane: Integer
}

process COUNT_LINES {
    input:
    tuple(meta: Sample, bam: Path)

    output:
    stdout()

    script:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    printf &#x27;%s\\t&#x27; &#x27;${meta.sample}&#x27;
    wc -l &lt; &#x27;${bam}&#x27;
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
}

workflow {
    COUNT_LINES(nextflow.Channel.fromStore(selection: &#x27;&lt;selection address&gt;&#x27;, records: true))
        .view()
}
```

